{"id":"work-labour","topic":"work","title":"Job markets differ by several points between Canadian cities, and the year-on-year moves are mostly small","question":"Which cities have tighter labour markets, and how are conditions changing?","dek":"Labour Force Survey rates for 26 metro areas, with the smaller places shown on their published geography.","findings":[{"id":"labour-rate-spread","kind":"observation","headline":"Unemployment ranges from 4.0% in Sherbrooke to 9.3% in Kelowna (three months to 2026-09)","detail":"Across 26 CMAs and CMA parts the seasonally adjusted three-month average unemployment rate runs from 4.0% (Sherbrooke) to 9.3% (Kelowna); the middle is about 6.5%. Employment rates (share of everyone 15+ who is working) range from 57.5% in London to 66.8% in Calgary, which also reflects age structure and who is in the labour force. The Ottawa-Gatineau halves differ: Ontario part 7.2%, Quebec part 6.3%. Differences of one point or less between nearby cities are often within sampling error.","stat":{"value":5.3,"unit":"pp","label":"Highest minus lowest unemployment rate","period":"2026-09"},"placeIds":["sherbrooke","kelowna","calgary","london","ottawa","gatineau"],"metricId":"lfs_unemployment_rate","sourceIds":["statcan-14-10-0459-01"],"calc":"Table 14-10-0459-01, seasonally adjusted, Estimate, REF_DATE 2026-09; unemployment rate and employment rate for the 26 CMA / Ottawa-Gatineau part places; territories, Charlottetown (provincial) and Brandon (regional, unadjusted) excluded from the ranking."},{"id":"labour-yoy-change","kind":"calculation","headline":"Compared with a year earlier, unemployment fell in 16 of 26 places and rose in 10, but only 5 changes are clearly larger than sampling noise","detail":"Changes beyond 1.96 standard errors: Windsor -3.1 pp, Toronto -1.9 pp, Calgary -1.8 pp, Winnipeg -1.4 pp, London +2.1 pp. Every other change is small relative to the survey's sampling error and should not be read as a trend. Both values are three-month moving averages, so a one-month swing is dampened and consecutive months overlap.","stat":{"value":2.1,"unit":"pp","label":"Largest significant change (London)","period":"2026-09"},"placeIds":["windsor","toronto","calgary","winnipeg","london"],"metricId":"lfs_unemployment_rate_change_1y","sourceIds":["statcan-14-10-0459-01"],"calc":"change = rate(2026-09) - rate(2025-09) from 14-10-0459-01 (SA, 3-month moving average); significant if |change| > 1.96 x published standard error of year-over-year change; 26 CMA / part places."}],"charts":[{"id":"unemployment-ranked","type":"bar_ranked","title":"Unemployment rate by city, three months to 2026-09","subtitle":"Seasonally adjusted three-month moving average, population 15+","unit":"percent","series":[{"id":"ur","label":"Unemployment rate","points":[{"x":"Kelowna","y":9.3,"placeId":"kelowna"},{"x":"London","y":8.4,"placeId":"london"},{"x":"Kitchener–Waterloo","y":7.7,"placeId":"kitchener-waterloo"},{"x":"Moncton","y":7.6,"placeId":"moncton"},{"x":"Windsor","y":7.3,"placeId":"windsor"},{"x":"Ottawa","y":7.2,"placeId":"ottawa"},{"x":"Toronto","y":6.9,"placeId":"toronto"},{"x":"Hamilton","y":6.9,"placeId":"hamilton"},{"x":"Edmonton","y":6.9,"placeId":"edmonton"},{"x":"Montréal","y":6.8,"placeId":"montreal"},{"x":"Abbotsford–Mission","y":6.8,"placeId":"abbotsford-mission"},{"x":"St. John's","y":6.6,"placeId":"st-johns"},{"x":"Vancouver","y":6.5,"placeId":"vancouver"},{"x":"Regina","y":6.4,"placeId":"regina"},{"x":"Gatineau","y":6.3,"placeId":"gatineau"},{"x":"Calgary","y":6.3,"placeId":"calgary"},{"x":"Greater Sudbury","y":6.0,"placeId":"sudbury"},{"x":"Fredericton","y":6.0,"placeId":"fredericton"},{"x":"Saskatoon","y":5.9,"placeId":"saskatoon"},{"x":"Halifax","y":5.8,"placeId":"halifax"},{"x":"Saint John","y":5.6,"placeId":"saint-john"},{"x":"Victoria","y":5.3,"placeId":"victoria"},{"x":"Winnipeg","y":5.0,"placeId":"winnipeg"},{"x":"Thunder Bay","y":4.3,"placeId":"thunder-bay"},{"x":"Québec City","y":4.3,"placeId":"quebec-city"